file=Comms & PR/herald/custom/media-training/SKILL.md · h=<!--\nCustom skill — built from scratch. NOT in the original catalog (which listed only\nmedia-relations + press-kit for herald); added per §2 roster expansion at operator\nsign-off.\n\nSource content: T.J. Walker media-training corpus + Boyd Neil / Susan Bassett Media\nTraining Handbook + SPJ (Society of Professional Journalists) on-record standards +\nPoynter Institute media-education materials.\n\nNo genericization strip needed — media-training frameworks are public professional\npractice, no VYON-branded content.\n-->\nname: media-training\ntype: custom\nstatus: built from scratch (added per §2 roster expansion beyond catalog's 2-skill floor)\nsources_referenced:\n- "Walker, T. J. Media Training Success + Public Speaking for Success (multiple editions since 2010). Practitioner-operator per §8.9 — media-training coach for corporate executives; extensive published-book corpus."\n- "Neil, Boyd & Bassett, Susan. Media Training Handbook. Practitioner reference."\n- "Society of Professional Journalists (SPJ) — Code of Ethics + on-record / off-record / background / deep-background standards. Institutional source per §8.8."\n- "Poynter Institute — media-education materials on interview conduct + spokesperson standards. Institutional source per §8.8."\n- "Scott, David Meerman (2020). The New Rules of Marketing and PR. Wiley. Already cited in herald's media-relations and identity file; §8.9 extract-once-use-twice."\nfulfills_catalog_entry: n/a (part of herald's expanded roster beyond catalog per §2)\ngenericization_notes:\n- "No VYON-branded content — media-training frameworks are public professional practice."\nassigned_agent: herald (Comms & PR / Lead — PR & Media)\nportable: true\ndate_online: 2026-07-31\ntier: 3\ndescription: Spokesperson preparation for high-stakes media interviews — message-map discipline (3-message max), bridging technique (ABC: Acknowledge, Bridge, Communicate), hostile-Q handling, on-record / off-record / background / deep-background boundaries per SPJ standards, and dry-run rehearsal protocol. Trigger on "prep for interview", "media training for", "spokesperson prep", "how do I handle this reporter question", "on-record vs off-record", "bridging technique", or "rehearse the interview".\ntriggers:\n- prep for interview\n- media training for\n- spokesperson prep\n- how do I handle this reporter question\n- on-record vs off-record\n- bridging technique\n- rehearse the interview\n- message map for\n · in:=This skill packages the standard media-training frameworks (T.J. Walker + Neil/Bassett\npractitioner corpus + SPJ on-record standards + Poynter Institute institutional\nmaterials) into herald's operational entry point for spokesperson preparation. When a\n`media-relations` pitch lands and produces an interview — or when an inbound reporter\ninquiry escalates to a live conversation — the spokesperson needs preparation. Without\nit, even well-prepared executives freeze on hostile questions, drift from key messages,\nor accidentally cross on-record boundaries.\n\nAdded per §2 roster expansion — not in the original catalog. The catalog covered\nmedia-relations (pitching) + press-kit (canonical content) for herald; media-training\nfills the obvious gap between "pitch landed → interview scheduled" and "interview\nhappens well."\n · p:=Prevents four failure modes that show up when spokespeople go into interviews unprepared:\n\n1. **Message drift.** Spokesperson enters with 8 things to say; reporter picks up on\nthe 3rd and drives the whole interview around a tangential point. Coverage doesn't\nreflect the intended story. The 3-message-max discipline (Principle 1) prevents this.\n2. **Freeze on hostile questions.** Reporter asks the difficult question (competitor\ncomparison, past mistake, tough industry critique); spokesperson stammers, agrees\nwith the framing implicitly, or lies. Bridging technique (Phase 3) and hostile-Q\ndrill (Phase 4) train the response.\n3. **On-record boundary breach.** Spokesperson says something in what they thought was\noff-record but was actually on-the-record; or references confidential information\nthinking the reporter wouldn't use it. SPJ-standard boundary clarity (Phase 5)\nprevents this.\n4. **Fact error live.** Spokesperson misstates a company stat, product feature, or\nexecutive credential during the interview. Post-interview corrections become\ncrisis-comms events (route to beacon). Dry-run rehearsal (Phase 6) catches these\nbefore they ship.\n\nherald uses this skill whenever media-relations produces an interview that matters —\ntypically for founders, executives, or subject-matter experts speaking to reporters\ncovering something material to the business.\n · wu:=Trigger on:\n\n- "Prep for interview with [reporter / publication]"\n- "Media training for [spokesperson]"\n- "Spokesperson prep for [event / topic]"\n- "How do I handle this reporter question about [X]"\n- "On-record vs off-record" / "what's the difference between background and deep background"\n- "Bridging technique" / "how do I redirect this question to my message"\n- "Rehearse the interview" / "run a mock interview"\n- "Message map for [topic / interview]"\n- Handoff from `media-relations` when a pitch lands and an interview is scheduled\n\nDo NOT use for:\n\n- **Reporter outreach / pitching** → `media-relations` (custom, herald — sibling). This\nskill is spokesperson-side; media-relations is reporter-side.\n- **Press-release drafting / press-kit content** → `press-kit` (custom, herald — sibling).\n- **Crisis-comms training** (for actual live crisis) → `crisis-comms` (custom, beacon —\nComms & PR sibling). Media-training preps spokespeople for planned interviews; crisis\nmoments are beacon's scope (though this skill's frameworks — bridging, hostile-Q,\nboundary discipline — inform crisis-comms).\n- **Public speaking / keynote coaching** (not a media interview) → out of scope; route\nto operator or an external coach.\n- **Individual mental-health signals during prep** — HARD BOUNDARY escalation to manager\n+ HR Ops + EAP per Universal Principle 3 (inherited).\n · st:/ Protocol=The media-training preparation sequence:\n\n```\n1. INTERVIEW SCOPE INTAKE\nReporter + publication + topic + format (in-person / phone / video / written Q&A) +\ndate + duration + on-record status (default: on-record unless negotiated otherwise).\n\n2. MESSAGE MAP (3-messages MAX)\n3 key messages the spokesperson wants coverage to reflect. Each message has:\na. The message itself (1 sentence)\nb. 2-3 proof points (data / example / third-party validation)\nc. Anticipated challenge to the message (hostile-Q anchor)\nSourced from press-kit canonical library (single source of truth).\n\n3. BRIDGING DRILL (from ANY question → to KEY message)\nABC formula (Walker + Neil/Bassett):\nA: ACKNOWLEDGE the question briefly (never dismiss it)\nB: BRIDGE with a transition ("What's important here is..." / "The bigger point\nis..." / "Let me put this in context...")\nC: COMMUNICATE the key message\nPractice bridging from 5-10 anticipated questions to each of the 3 messages.\n\n4. HOSTILE-Q DRILL\nAnticipated hostile questions (competitor comparison; past failure; industry\ncritique; regulatory concern; personal-life question). For each: recognize the\npattern (loaded premise? false dichotomy? gotcha frame?) → reframe (don't accept\nthe premise) → bridge to a truthful key message.\nRule: don't lie. Rule: don't accept a false premise. Rule: reframe honestly.\n\n5. ON-RECORD BOUNDARY CLARITY (SPJ standards)\nConfirm status BEFORE the interview starts:\n- ON-RECORD (default): everything usable + attributable\n- OFF-RECORD: not usable in any form\n- BACKGROUND: usable, not attributable to spokesperson\n- DEEP BACKGROUND: usable, not attributable to org\nOff-record and background require EXPLICIT reporter agreement BEFORE the statement\nis made — you can't retroactively make on-record content off-record.\n\n6. DRY-RUN REHEARSAL\nSomeone plays the reporter. Ideally recorded (audio at minimum). Reviewed together\nafterwards. 30-60 minute rehearsal for a 20-minute interview.\nFocus areas: message discipline (did all 3 messages get in?); bridging fluency\n(natural or forced?); hostile-Q handling (composed or defensive?); fact accuracy\n(any errors?); on-record boundaries (any accidental over-shares?).\n\n7. INTERVIEW EXECUTION SUPPORT\nJust-before briefing (15-30 min pre-interview): confirm scope; refresh message map;\nconfirm on-record status; confirm reporter's likely angles from recent coverage;\nsingle-page cheatsheet ready.\n\n8. POST-INTERVIEW DEBRIEF\nSpokesperson + herald review: what worked; what went off-message; any factual\ncorrections needed; any on-record boundary near-miss; any follow-up media-relations\nshould send (materials the reporter asked for).\nFeeds forward into press-kit's Q&A library (§Instructions Phase 1) — anticipated\nquestions library learns from actual interviews.\n```\n · ### Phase 1 — Interview scope intake=Confirm the specifics BEFORE building the message map:\n\n- **Reporter + publication.** Coordinate with `media-relations` for the reporter research\nmemo (recent coverage, beat, publication constraints).\n- **Topic.** Specific angle the reporter is pursuing. If unclear, ask media-relations\nto confirm with reporter — a wide-open topic produces unfocused prep.\n- **Format.** In-person / phone / video / written Q&A. Written Q&A is the safest (edit\nbefore submission); video is the most demanding (visual composure matters).\n- **Date + duration.** How much time to prep; how long the interview runs.\n- **On-record status.** Default is on-record. Off-record or background requires reporter\nagreement, negotiated by media-relations BEFORE the interview scheduling completes.\n · ### Phase 2 — Build the message map (3-messages MAX)=**Hard rule: 3 messages maximum.** More than 3 = message drift is guaranteed;\nspokesperson can't hold that many in working memory during a live interview. Fewer than\n3 = leaving air-time coverage on the table.\n\nFor each message:\n\n- **The message itself in 1 sentence.** Plain English. Avoids jargon that reporter's\naudience wouldn't understand. Passes the "if this were the headline, would we be\nhappy?" test.\n- **2-3 proof points.** Data point + real example + third-party validation (customer\nquote, industry ranking, published research). Sourced from press-kit's canonical\nlibrary (fact sheet + case-study archive + coverage archive).\n- **Anticipated challenge.** What's the strongest hostile-question anchor to this\nmessage? Prepared response ready.\n\n**Source discipline:** every proof point traces to the press-kit fact sheet + verified\nsources. No fabricated stats per Universal Principle 1 (inherited) + press-kit\nPrinciple 2. If a message needs a proof point that doesn't exist in verified form —\neither find the proof or drop the message.\n · ### Phase 3 — Bridging drill (ABC formula)=Bridging is the skill of moving from ANY question to a KEY message truthfully.\n\n**A: Acknowledge** — briefly. Don't dismiss the question. Don't over-agree with hostile\npremises. Simple acknowledgment: "That's an important question." / "I understand why\nyou're asking." / "Let me address that directly."\n\n**B: Bridge** — transition to your terrain. Standard bridges:\n- "What's important here is..."\n- "The bigger point is..."\n- "Let me put this in context..."\n- "That's part of a broader pattern where..."\n- "The way we think about this is..."\n\n**C: Communicate** — deliver the key message with proof.\n\n**Drill discipline:** practice bridging from 5-10 anticipated questions to each of the\n3 messages. Not every question requires bridging — if the reporter's question directly\nmatches one of your key messages, answer directly. Bridging is for tangential or hostile\nquestions, not every question.\n\n**Anti-pattern check:** bridging is a TECHNIQUE, not evasion. If the reporter's\nquestion deserves a direct answer (e.g., a factual clarification), give it. Over-bridging\nlooks like political-non-answer and damages credibility.\n · ### Phase 4 — Hostile-Q drill=Anticipated hostile-question patterns:\n\n- **Loaded premise:** "Given that your product has failed to solve X, how do you..."\n→ recognize the premise ("failed"); refuse it; reframe ("Here's what we've actually\nobserved...").\n- **False dichotomy:** "Are you A or B?" → recognize the false framing; introduce the\nactual answer ("The framing here isn't A vs B — it's..."; then explain).\n- **Gotcha frame:** "You said X two years ago — has anything changed?" → acknowledge\nthe prior statement honestly; explain what evolved and why.\n- **Competitor comparison:** don't disparage the competitor; reframe to your own\nstrengths and your customer's outcomes.\n- **Past failure question:** acknowledge honestly; describe what was learned; bridge\nto current improved state (if truthful).\n- **Personal question:** decline politely + bridge back to the professional topic. Not\nevery question deserves an answer.\n\n**Never lie. Never accept a false premise. Always reframe honestly.** The hostile-Q\ndrill trains composure under pressure, not deception. A spokesperson who lies gets\ncaught; the org's credibility damage exceeds any short-term win.\n · ### Phase 5 — On-record boundary clarity (SPJ standards)=**Before the interview starts** (not during), confirm status with the reporter:\n\n- **On-record (default).** Everything spokesperson says is usable + attributable by name.\n- **Off-record.** Not usable in any form. Requires explicit "this next part is off-record,\nagreed?" + reporter agreement BEFORE the statement is made. Reporters may decline\nto accept off-record; some publications don't allow it.\n- **Background.** Usable, not attributable to the individual spokesperson. "A company\nsource said..." Requires explicit negotiation.\n- **Deep background.** Usable, not attributable to the organization at all. Rare in\ncorporate media; typical in government / political comms. Requires explicit negotiation\nand mutual clarity on what "deep background" means to THIS reporter (definitions vary).\n\n**Load-bearing rules:**\n\n- You cannot retroactively make on-record content off-record. If it came out of your\nmouth on-record, it's usable.\n- If the status is unclear, assume on-record.\n- Chit-chat before / after the interview is typically on-record too. Don't say anything\nduring "just casual" moments you wouldn't want quoted.\n- Written communications (email, DM, text with a reporter) are on-record by default\nunless explicitly negotiated otherwise.\n · ### Phase 6 — Dry-run rehearsal=30-60 minutes of dry-run for a 20-minute interview. Ideally:\n\n- **Someone plays the reporter** (herald, or a member of the team who can adopt the\nactual reporter's likely approach based on their recent coverage). NOT the CEO or the\nspokesperson themselves.\n- **Recorded** (audio at minimum; video for on-camera interviews).\n- **Reviewed together afterwards** — spokesperson listens to their own responses,\nidentifies where message discipline slipped, where bridging felt forced, where\nhostile-Q handling was defensive, where fact accuracy needs correction.\n\n**Focus review on:**\n\n1. Message discipline — did all 3 messages get delivered clearly?\n2. Bridging fluency — did the bridges sound natural or forced?\n3. Hostile-Q handling — composed or defensive?\n4. Fact accuracy — any errors that need correction before the real interview?\n5. On-record boundaries — any accidental over-shares during rehearsal that would be\nover-shares in the real interview?\n\nIf a dry-run reveals systematic issues (spokesperson unable to hold 3 messages;\nhostile-Q handling fundamentally off), consider deferring the interview or substituting\na different spokesperson. A weak interview does more damage than a delayed / declined\none.\n · ### Phase 7 — Interview execution support=Just-before-interview briefing (15-30 minutes before):\n\n- Refresh the 3 key messages\n- Confirm on-record status\n- Confirm reporter's likely angles from their recent coverage\n- Deliver single-page cheatsheet: 3 messages + top 5 anticipated Qs + 3 stats to have ready\n\n**During the interview:** herald typically doesn't sit in (reporters prefer 1:1); herald\nis available immediately after.\n · ### Phase 8 — Post-interview debrief=Spokesperson + herald review (30 minutes, typically same-day):\n\n- What worked (message delivery, bridge that landed, hostile-Q handled well).\n- What went off-message (which message got less air-time; which tangent consumed too\nmuch time).\n- Any factual corrections needed — if so, route to `press-kit` + `media-relations` for\ncorrection outreach to reporter BEFORE the piece runs (Universal Principle 5 legal\nfence if the correction is material).\n- Any on-record boundary near-miss — surfaces the pattern for future prep.\n- Any follow-up media-relations should send (materials the reporter asked for during\nthe interview).\n\n**Feed forward to press-kit's Q&A library** — the anticipated-questions library learns\nfrom actual interviews.\n · of:=Each invocation produces one or more of:\n\n- **Message map** — 3 messages + proof points + anticipated challenges. Formatted for\nspokesperson memorization + reference during interview.\n- **Bridging drill script** — 5-10 questions × 3 messages = 15-30 practice bridges.\n- **Hostile-Q brief** — anticipated hostile-Q patterns + prepared reframes + underlying\nmessages to bridge to.\n- **On-record status confirmation** — one-line clarification with reporter (typically\nmedia-relations handles the negotiation; media-training confirms it in the briefing).\n- **Dry-run script** — questions for the mock-reporter role-play + review criteria.\n- **Just-before-interview cheatsheet** — single page: 3 messages + top 5 anticipated Qs\n+ 3 stats.\n- **Post-interview debrief memo** — what worked + what didn't + corrections needed +\nfollow-up materials + Q&A library updates.\n · pr:=1. **3-messages MAX.** More than 3 = drift guaranteed; fewer than 3 = leaving coverage\non the table. This is the load-bearing discipline — not a preference. (Walker; Neil &\nBassett — practitioner consensus.)\n2. **Bridging is a technique, not evasion.** Never lie. Never accept a false premise.\nReframe honestly. Over-bridging looks like political non-answer and damages\ncredibility.\n3. **Never lie or knowingly mislead.** A spokesperson who lies gets caught; the credibility\ndamage exceeds any short-term win. §0.5 applied at the interview surface.\n4. **On-record status is confirmed BEFORE the interview starts.** SPJ standard: no\nretroactive off-record; unclear status defaults to on-record; chit-chat is on-record.\n5. **Every proof point traces to press-kit canonical facts.** No fabricated stats; no\ninvented case studies; no misrepresented product features. Fact-check discipline\ninherited from press-kit Principle 2.\n6. **Dry-run before high-stakes interviews.** 30-60 minutes for a 20-minute interview.\nRecorded + reviewed. If dry-run reveals systematic issues, consider deferring or\nsubstituting spokesperson — a weak interview does more damage than a delayed one.\n7. **Not every question deserves an answer.** Personal questions can be politely declined\n+ bridged. Loaded-premise questions can be refused and reframed. Media-training\nspokespeople choose which questions to engage.\n8. **Individual mental-health signals during prep escalate immediately.** Universal\nPrinciple 3 (inherited). If a spokesperson surfaces personal distress during\npreparation (about the interview or unrelated), route to manager + HR Ops + EAP\nbefore continuing prep. Do NOT push a distressed spokesperson into a high-stakes\ninterview.\n9. **§0.6 flag.** Media-training frameworks (3-message max, ABC bridging, SPJ boundaries,\n30-60-min dry-run) are Tier B (canonical practitioner + institutional guidance cited\nbut not book-page-cited from `Agents/_books/`). Downgrade to Tier A when Walker\n*Media Training Success* + Neil & Bassett *Media Training Handbook* are placed and\na `Shared OS/logical/media_training.md` Route-D asset is built per §8.9.\n · f:=- **Spokesperson can't hold 3 messages in dry-run.** Consider reducing to 2 messages,\nor substituting a different spokesperson. Do NOT push into interview with an\nunder-prepared speaker.\n- **Reporter refuses off-record / background status the media-relations negotiated.**\nAssume on-record; brief the spokesperson to plan for on-record even if partial\nbackground was expected. Never assume the reporter's silence means agreement.\n- **Fact error surfaces during dry-run.** Fix it in the message map; re-run dry-run;\nupdate press-kit's fact sheet if the fact was previously stated incorrectly (route\nto press-kit for archive update).\n- **Hostile-Q spokesperson can't handle in rehearsal.** Rewrite the response; more\nbridging drill; if still not landing, consider defer / substitute or narrow the\ninterview topic.\n- **Interview topic is material non-public information** (unannounced M&A, financial\nrestatement, executive departure). Route to `press-kit` + operator + securities\ncounsel before the interview happens; the interview may need to be declined or\nrestricted in scope per Universal Principle 5 legal fence.\n- **Individual mental-health signal from spokesperson during prep.** STOP. Route per\nUniversal Principle 3 to manager + HR Ops + EAP. Do NOT push into interview.\n- **Reporter asks a question during the interview that spokesperson doesn't know.**\nPrepared response: "I don't have that at hand — I can follow up with you after the\ninterview." Route the follow-up to media-relations. Do NOT fabricate an answer\nunder pressure.\n- **Correction needed after the interview but before the piece runs.** Route to\npress-kit + media-relations for reporter outreach immediately. Corrections before\npublication are much cheaper than corrections after. If the correction is material,\nlegal counsel involved.\n · tb=\| Hands off to / from \| For \| Direction \| · tb=\|---\|---\|---\| · tb=\| `media-relations` (custom, herald — sibling) \| Interview scheduling + reporter research memo + follow-up materials post-interview \| Bidirectional — media-relations schedules; media-training preps; media-relations follows up \| · tb=\| `press-kit` (custom, herald — sibling) \| Message-map proof points sourced from press-kit canonical library; anticipated Q&A library updated post-interview \| Bidirectional — press-kit provides content; media-training feeds Q&A library back \| · tb=\| `pr-analytics` (custom, herald — sibling) \| Coverage tracking post-interview measures message-discipline outcome (did the coverage reflect the intended messages?) \| Downstream — pr-analytics measures the interview's coverage outcome \| · tb=\| `crisis-comms` (custom, beacon — Comms & PR sibling) \| Media-training's frameworks (bridging, hostile-Q, boundary discipline) inform crisis-comms spokesperson prep during live crisis moments \| Cross-cutting — same frameworks, different context \| · tb=\| `signal` (custom — Comms & PR sibling, Internal Comms) \| Internal spokesperson prep for all-hands meetings uses similar bridging + message-map discipline (though not on-record boundaries) \| Cross-cutting — signal borrows the frameworks \| · tb=\| `echo` (Executive Office / Investor & external comms) \| Executive-voice prep for investor briefings uses similar discipline; echo owns the artifact-side, media-training owns the delivery-side \| Cross-department coordination \| · tb=\| `spark / lena / weave / muse` (Brand Studio) \| Brand voice consistency — messages must sound like the brand voice \| Cross-department \| · tb=\| `board` (Governance) \| Material-non-public-info topics require board / legal approval before interview happens \| Escalation \| · tb=\| Operator + securities counsel \| Legally-sensitive interview topics; material non-public info \| Escalation — Universal Principle 5 legal fence \| · tb=\| Manager + HR Ops + EAP \| Individual mental-health signal during prep — HARD BOUNDARY \| Escalation — Universal Principle 3 \| · tb=\| `Shared OS: verification-before-completion` \| Evidence gate on every message-map + dry-run script + post-interview debrief \| Cross-cutting \| · r(/:References (public / verifiable)=- [Society of Professional Journalists — Code of Ethics + attribution standards](https://www.spj.org/ethicscode.asp)\n- [Poynter Institute — media education materials](https://www.poynter.org/)\n- [T.J. Walker — media-training author + resources](https://www.tjwalker.com/)\n- [Cision — media training tips (institutional-adjacent supplement)](https://www.cision.com/resources/)\n- [PRSA — Public Relations Society of America — professional standards](https://www.prsa.org/)\n